  <h1>How to Negotiate Salary</h1>
  <p><em>Reviewed by Aspen Lockwood (AL), Editor-in-Chief. Updated May 2026.</em></p>
  <h2>Step 1: Research your market rate</h2><p>Use Glassdoor, Levels.fyi (tech), LinkedIn Salary, Payscale, and industry surveys. Factor in your location, years of experience, specific skills, and the size/type of employer. Collect 5–10 data points. Your ask must be defensible with data.</p>
  <h2>Step 2: Avoid disclosing salary history</h2><p>Many states prohibit salary history questions. Even where legal, you are not obligated to answer. &ldquo;I&rsquo;d prefer to focus on what this role is worth to the company rather than my past compensation&rdquo; is a professional non-answer. Disclosing too early anchors the negotiation at your current salary, not your market value.</p>
  <h2>Step 3: Let the offer come to you</h2><p>Don&rsquo;t volunteer a number first. If asked for salary expectations before an offer: &ldquo;I&rsquo;m still learning about the role and responsibilities. Can you share the range budgeted for this position?&rdquo; This often gets you the range — negotiating information for free.</p>
  <h2>Step 4: Anchor high — then be quiet</h2><p>When you make your ask, state your number and stop talking. &ldquo;Based on my research and X years of [skill], I&rsquo;m targeting $Y.&rdquo; Silence after the ask is powerful — resist filling it. The first person to speak after a salary anchor typically moves toward the other&rsquo;s number.</p>
  <h2>Step 5: Negotiate total comp</h2><p>If base is constrained, negotiate: signing bonus, annual target bonus, equity/RSUs, vacation, remote flexibility, review cycle timing, or professional development budget. Total compensation is often more flexible than base.</p>
  <h2>Where to go from here</h2>
  <p>See <a href="/guides/salary-research-guide">salary research guide</a> and <a href="/guides/negotiating-job-offers">negotiating job offers</a>.</p>
